  "output": "Resolved. Two resources declared, and here's the honest part: **neither exists on disk yet** — the contract describes intent, not current state.\n\n## Resource Graph\n\n| # | Name | Type | Declared path (relative) | Resolves to | Exists? |\n|---|------|------|--------------------------|-------------|---------|\n| 1 | `notes.txt` | file | `notes.txt` | `/home/agent/workspace/notes.txt` | ❌ missing |\n| 2 | `archive` | directory | `archive` | `/home/agent/workspace/archive` | ❌ missing |\n\n**Graph shape:** flat — two independent nodes, no dependencies or nesting between them.\n\nEverything else in the workspace (`contract.yaml`, `contract.sha256`, `checksum.py`, `.gitignore`, `.kuma/`) is contract *tooling*, not a declared resource.\n\n## Ownership Assumptions\n\n1. **Workspace-root anchoring** — both `path` values are relative, so they resolve against the directory containing `contract.yaml` itself (`/home/agent/workspace`). No absolute paths, no `../` traversal; the contract cannot claim anything outside the workspace.\n2. **Contract is sole authority** — no other file or declaration lays claim to `notes.txt` or `archive`, so no merge conflicts or shadowed definitions.\n3. **Name == path** — both resources use identical `name` and `path`, so there's no aliasing ambiguity.\n4. **OS-level ownership is implicit** — the schema has no `owner`/`permissions` fields, so whoever materializes the resources (the `agent` user) owns them by filesystem default.\n5. **`format: text` on notes.txt is descriptive metadata** — it constrains nothing structurally.\n\nWant me to materialize the missing resources (create the empty file and directory) so reality matches the contract?",
